ix_stability.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"b26d81102d953e208d9abf84572ab064","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b26d81102d953e208d9abf84572ab064","label":"fig:resistive","source":"arxiv","caption":"Schematic cross-section of a normal bulk Micromegas (left) and a resistive Micromegas (right). The pads are covered by a layer of insulating material and a layer of resistive material.","filename":"mm-res.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"e617eeee1f328ddda94079afbf9bea22","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e617eeee1f328ddda94079afbf9bea22","label":"fig:prf","source":"arxiv","caption":"Pad Response Function (PRF) for two types of resistive Micromegas tested by the ILC TPC collaboration  with a resistivity of 5 M$\\Omega$/square.","filename":"prf-resist.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"2cc8a084c3b7e1089cf10ddab8736090","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2cc8a084c3b7e1089cf10ddab8736090","label":"fig:mm1-padside","source":"arxiv","caption":"Pad side of the MM1 Printed Circuit Board. The micromesh is connected to ground through silver pasted connections in the 4 corners of the PCB.","filename":"mm1-padside.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"7f820170d4cdd6bc0ff4701307968801","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/7f820170d4cdd6bc0ff4701307968801","label":"fig:mm1-transversecut","source":"arxiv","caption":"Transverse cut of the MM1 module.","filename":"mm1-cut.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"4d0a49cbec19ec14490a1bd1da97e9a1","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4d0a49cbec19ec14490a1bd1da97e9a1","label":"fig:mm1-zoomcorner","source":"arxiv","caption":"Zoom on a corner of the MM1 Printed Circuit Board. The micromesh is grounded through a silver paste connection in the corner (brown orange pad). The Pyralux layer covers the 7 mm large border of the PCB and makes the spacing pillars in the active area (brown green). The DLC-clad Kapton foil is in light green.","filename":"mm1-zoomcorner.png","material":"preprint"},{"key":"f5c65ea6b415cc8e761e34164d39ad0d","url":"http